st in the design canvas for the evaluator to see. With clipping ON and no scroll, rows 5-10 are hidden below. The evaluator may view the frame in Figma design mode where content is clipped — they can't see rows 5-10. That risks failing \"Exercises 1-10\" coverage check!\n\nOptions: (a) make frame taller to show all content (frame height = content height ~1332). But brief says canvas 414×896. \"02_Home, 03_Warm_Up and 04_Activity may scroll vertically\" — this implies the frames can scroll, meaning the content extends beyond 896 but the frame is 896 with vertical scrolling enabled in the prototype.\n\nBest approach: make 03 a proper scrollable screen:\n- Frame 414×896, clipsContent ON.\n- Inner scroll container frame with overflow scroll? In Figma, prototype scrolling works via overflowBehavior on frames: set a child frame (e.g., \"Content\") with fixed height and overflowBehavior SCROLL_VERTICAL, containing the long content. The tab bar stays outside the scroll container, pinned at bottom.\n\nLet me restructure 03: create a \"Scroll\" frame at y=0 height 800 (or 896-96), containing hero + content. Move hero, title, tags, desc, exercises into it. Set scroll.overflowBehavior = \"SCROLL_VERTICAL\". The scroll frame height should be its full content height (e.g., 1356) and the parent clips it... Actually in Figma, for prototype scroll: the scroll frame itself must have fixed height that fits the viewport region, and its content exceeds its height, with clipsContent true and overflowBehavior SCROLL_VERTICAL. Wait no — in Figma, you make a frame with content larger than the frame and set \"Overflow scrolling: Vertical\" in prototype settings. The frame's height = viewport height. Children beyond the frame height are clipped but scrollable.\n\nSo structure: 03 frame (414×896, clip) → children: ScrollArea (414×800, y=0, overflowBehavior VERTICAL, clips) containing hero/title/.../rows (content height ~1356), plus TabBar at y=800 (outside ScrollArea), plus status bar overlay.\n\nBut wait — status bar is dark text on the light blue hero. Currently using StatusBar/Light (white glyphs). If hero scrolls, status bar stays fixed on top — fine as overlay.\n\nHmm, but the ScrollArea clips the hero... the hero is at top so fine.\n\nLet me restructure:\n1. Create scroll frame \"Scroll\" at (0,0) size 414×800, clipsContent true.\n2. Move hero (300h) + all content except tab bar into it.\n3. scroll.overflowBehavior = \"SCROLL_VERTICAL\".\n4. Content extends to y=1332 in scroll coords — same as now (hero at 0).\n5. Tab bar at y 800.\n\nWait, tab bar height 96, 800+96=896. ✓.\n\nAlso the status bar light overlay stays on top, above scroll area.\n\nSimilarly for 02 and 04, content fits (798 < 800), no scroll needed.\n\nAlso for 02, the category strip is horizontal scroll. It's a child frame wider than viewport. Set strip.overflowBehavior = \"SCROLL_HORIZONTAL\" — nice touch for prototype.\n\nAlso 02 main frame doesn't scroll (content ends at 788). OK.\n\nFor 03, move nodes: hero, Warm up title, tags (2), description, Exercises label, 10 rows → into Scroll frame. Keep tab bar + status bar outside.\n\nNote: status bar currently appended after hero in f children — it's on top of everything (index order: hero, ..., sb, title, tags...). Actually sb was added after hero, then title etc added after → title etc are above sb in z. That's fine (they don't overlap). After restructure, sb should be appended last (on top).\n\nWait — actually currently on 03: children order: hero, sb, title \"Warm up\", tags, desc, \"Exercises\", rows×10, tabbar. The status bar is between hero and title — hero covers it? No: sb comes AFTER hero in children, so sb renders ON TOP of hero. Good, that's what we want.\n\nHmm wait, earlier I did `f.insertChild(f.children.length, sb)` to move it to top. ✓.\n\nRestructure plan for 03:\n```\nconst scroll = figma.createFrame();\nf.appendChild(scroll); // then move before tabbar\nscroll.name = \"Scroll\";\nscroll.resize(414, 800);\nscroll.x =